  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday through next Saturday)\nIssued at 116 PM CDT Sat Oct 24 2026\n\nThe start of next week leans on the dry side, but active troughing \nover the western half of the US and the nearby subtropical jet \nstream will gradually expand over the area over the course of the \nweek and introduce opportunities for rain chances. The passage of a \npair of troughs over the Great Plains will help pull a fetch of \nmoist air into South Central Texas on Tuesday. The synoptic forcing \nis weak, but the slight warm/moist advection leads to 20-40 percent \nrain chances Tuesday in the form of isolated showers, favoring the \nCoastal Plains and Rio Grande Plains northwestward to the Edwards \nPlateau. Overall amounts from these rains skew light in the latest \nsynoptic guidance with models projecting areal totals of a few \ntenths of an inch.\n\nA brief round of shortwave ridging Wednesday suggests brief \nintermission from synoptically driven activity, though continued \nonshore flow keeps the door open for isolated to widely scattered \ncoastal/seabreeze showers during the day with rain chances of 20 to \n45 percent that afternoon and evening.\n\nFollowing these lower profile opportunities for rain, the weather \npattern evolves to a more conducive one with better chances for \nscattered to more widespread rains later in the week. Strengthening \nmid- to upper-level flow aloft, along with several nodes of \nvorticity embedded within the subtropical jet, are expected to pull \na larger plume overlapping moisture from the Gulf and eastern \nPacific. Most models involve remnant moisture and perhaps some \nvorticity from Hurricane Vance in this plume as it gets sheared by \nthe aforementioned jet. Owing to a lack of organized vorticity, \ncurrent ensembles generally don't show a front or otherwise enhanced \nbaroclinicity to concentrate this moisture. This tends to favor a \nbroader distribution of showers and storms fanning out over South \nCentral Texas as activity pushes south to north. The rain chances \naccompanying this pattern pick up Thursday afternoon, and there is \ngood consensus among the dynamical and ML-based models that those \nchances should peak late Thursday into Friday. The peak rain \nchances are slightly higher over the Rio Grande Plains and \nsouthern Edwards Plateau (above 70 percent) where the strongest \nmoisture flux is anticipated (near 500 kg/m/s^2 of integrated \nvapor transport on the latest ENS), but rain chances across the \nentire area are above 40 percent. At this time this rainfall looks\nbroadly beneficial in magnitude with the heavy rain signal \nlooking low across the current guidance. Rain chances linger into \nSaturday, but chances begin decreasing as vorticity proceeds past \nthe area and the flow aloft becomes more zonal.\n\nThe broad troughing over the western US will help keep temperatures \ngenerally stable this upcoming week, varying mainly based on cloud \ncover and rain. Highs are forecast to stay in the upper 70s to mid \n80s for most of the area, with lows responding to the regional \nmoisture by staying in the low to mid 60s.",
